# SPDX-FileCopyrightText: 2024-2026 Fabian Januszewski
# SPDX-License-Identifier: LGPL-3.0-only WITH LicenseRef-CUDA-exception
#
# The whole notebook tour (01_ ... 10_) as one notebook: the cells of the 10 files in order.
# Open it in the notebook (Open notebook...) or run it with hacs --script tour_all.oc.

# %% [md] 01 -- the space and the coefficients
# $\Gamma=\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z})$ acts on the upper half plane $\mathfrak{H}$. The quotient $\Gamma\backslash\mathfrak{H}$ is an orbifold: a
# disc with two cone points, where the stabilisers are $\mathbb{Z}/4$ (at $i$) and $\mathbb{Z}/6$ (at $e^{2\pi i/3}$); the element $-1$
# acts trivially. HACS works with a finite triangulation of this quotient together with its isotropy groups.
#
# > **Coefficients.** For a prime $\ell$ and $n\ge0$ the coefficient system is the representation
# > $$V_n = \mathrm{Sym}^n\,\mathbb{F}_\ell^2,\qquad \dim V_n = n+1,$$
# > of $\Gamma$ (homogeneous polynomials of degree $n$ in two variables). Here $n=10$ and $\ell=691$, so $\dim V_{10}=11$.
#
# In the notebook every `display(...)` is rendered (TeX, tables); in a script it prints the canonical text.

# %% The model of SL_2(Z)\H
# The coefficient ring is the default F_ell, stated here so that the file runs in any order in one session (08 sets Z).
set coefficient_ring = "F_ell";
M = sl2z_model();
display(M);
assert(M.vertices == 7 and M.dims == [7, 11, 5], "7 vertices, 11 edges, 5 triangles");

# %% The coefficients Sym^10 over F_691
L = symmetric_power(M, 10, 691);
display(L);
assert(L.d_V == 11 and L.n + 1 == L.d_V, "dim Sym^10 = 11");

# %% [md] Reading the output
# | Mathematics | What HACS computed, and how |
# |---|---|
# | A triangulation of $\Gamma\backslash\mathfrak{H}$ (compactified by a boundary circle: a disc, Euler characteristic 1), and the two
#   finite stabilisers $\mathbb{Z}/4$, $\mathbb{Z}/6$. |
#   `sl2z_model()`: the 7-vertex model `sl2z_7vertex` (7 vertices, 11 edges, 5 triangles), with its exact tessellation tables.
#   Everything later is computed from this finite object. |
# | The local system $V_{10}=\mathrm{Sym}^{10}\mathbb{F}_{691}^2$ on the orbifold. |
#   `symmetric_power(M, 10, 691)`: a `LocalSystem` with `d_V = 11` (the rank) and its monodromy relations along the edges. |

# %% [md] 02 -- cohomology, and the boundary exact sequence
# For $\Gamma=\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z})$ HACS computes $H^q(\Gamma, V_n)$ from the triangulated model of step 01.
#
# > **Eichler–Shimura.** For even $n>0$ and $\ell>n+1$,
# > $$\dim_{\mathbb{F}_\ell} H^1(\Gamma, \mathrm{Sym}^n\mathbb{F}_\ell^2) = 2\dim S_{n+2} + 1,\qquad H^0 = H^2 = 0 .$$
# > For $n=10$ (weight 12), $S_{12}=\mathbb{C}\Delta$, so $\dim H^1 = 3$.
#
# `cohomology(L)` returns a **handle**: nothing is computed until a value is needed. In the notebook a bare `H` shows the handle as
# "declared" with a compute button; `display(H)` forces it.

# %% Setting and model
# The coefficient ring is the default F_ell, stated here so that the file runs in any order in one session (08 sets Z).
set coefficient_ring = "F_ell";
M = sl2z_model();

# %% A handle: declared, not computed
H = cohomology(symmetric_power(M, 10, 691));
H

# %% display forces the handle
display(H);
assert(H.dims == [0, 3, 0], "Eichler-Shimura at n = 10");

# %% [md] The boundary and the short exact sequence
# The Borel–Serre boundary of $\Gamma\backslash\mathfrak{H}$ is a circle, and restriction $r$ to it gives the long exact sequence of the
# pair, $H^\bullet_c$ being cohomology with compact support:
#
# $$0\to H^0 \xrightarrow{r} H^0_\partial \xrightarrow{\delta} H^1_c \xrightarrow{\iota} H^1 \xrightarrow{r} H^1_\partial \xrightarrow{\delta} H^2_c\to0,\qquad \dim:\ 0,\ 1,\ 3,\ 3,\ 1,\ 0 .$$
#
# The image of $\iota$ is the interior cohomology $H^1_!$ (the cusp form $\Delta$, once per sign), so
#
# > $$0 \longrightarrow H^1_! \longrightarrow H^1 \xrightarrow{\;r\;} H^1_\partial \longrightarrow 0,\qquad \dim = 2,\ 3,\ 1 .$$
#
# Mod 691 this sequence of Hecke modules does not split: that is Ramanujan's congruence (steps 03–05, and the showcases).

# %% The boundary, the cone and its long exact sequence
B = boundary(H);
C = cone(H);
display(B.dims, C.dims, C.les);
assert(B.dims == [1, 1] and C.dims == [0, 3, 0] and C.les_exact, "dim H_bd = (1, 1), dim H_c = (0, 3, 0), the LES is exact");
assert(C.H1_shriek == 2, "dim H^1_! = 2");

# %% A small prime sees more
display(cohomology(symmetric_power(M, 20, 5)));
assert(cohomology(symmetric_power(M, 20, 5)).dims == [1, 4, 0], "(20, 5): H^0 != 0");

# %% [md] Reading the output
# | Mathematics | What HACS computed, and how |
# |---|---|
# | $\dim H^\bullet = (0,3,0)$ over $\mathbb{F}_{691}$, as Eichler–Shimura predicts. |
#   `cohomology(L)` is lazy; `display` computes it (type `Cohomology`, typeset by the page). |
# | The six-term sequence with dimensions $0,1,3,3,1,0$; $\dim H^1_!=\operatorname{rk}\iota=2$. |
#   `cone(H).les`: one row per term, `term` (`Hc` $=H_c$, `H_bulk` $=H$, `H_bd` $=H_\partial$), `q`, `dim`, `rank_in` / `rank_out` (the
#   ranks of the maps into and out of the term) and `exact` ($\texttt{rank\_in}+\texttt{rank\_out}=\dim$). `H1_shriek` $=\dim\ker r^1$.
#   [C] `sec:cone`, `thm:conewelldef`; validation `HeckeConeValidation`. |
# | At $(n,\ell)=(20,5)$: $\dim H^\bullet=(1,4,0)$. Mod a small prime the invariants of $\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{F}_\ell)$ on $\mathrm{Sym}^n$
#   can be non-zero ($20=5\cdot4$ is a Dickson degree). |
#   `cohomology(symmetric_power(M, 20, 5))`; step 06 (the Dickson sequence) explains the jump. |

# %% [md] 03 -- Hecke operators as matrices
# The Hecke operator $T_p$ acts on $H^1=H^1(\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z}),\mathrm{Sym}^{10}\mathbb{F}_{691}^2)$. By Eichler–Shimura its eigenvalues are
# $\tau(2)=-24$ on the two-dimensional cusp part ($\Delta$, both signs) and $1+2^{11}=2049$ on the Eisenstein line. Mod 691 they agree:
#
# > $$-24\equiv 2049 \equiv 667 \pmod{691},\qquad \operatorname{char}(T_2\,|\,H^1) = (X+24)^3 = X^3+72X^2+346X+4 \in\mathbb{F}_{691}[X].$$
#
# This is Ramanujan's congruence at $p=2$.

# %% Setting, model and cohomology
# The coefficient ring is the default F_ell, stated here so that the file runs in any order in one session (08 sets Z).
set coefficient_ring = "F_ell";
M = sl2z_model();
H = cohomology(symmetric_power(M, 10, 691));

# %% T_2 on H^1 and its characteristic polynomial
T2 = hecke(H, 2);
display(T2.A(1));
display(charpoly(T2.A(1)));
f = poly(691, [24, 1]);
assert(charpoly(T2.A(1)) == mul(f, mul(f, f)), "char(T_2) = (X + 24)^3 over F_691");
assert((2049 + 24) % 691 == 0 and 2049 % 691 == 667, "1 + 2^11 = -24 = 667 mod 691");

# %% The operator handle
# The operator handle itself: its matrices per degree and their characteristic polynomials.
display(T2);

# %% [md] Reading the output
# | Mathematics | What HACS computed, and how |
# |---|---|
# | $T_2|_{H^1}=\begin{pmatrix}667&0&0\\0&667&0\\23&0&667\end{pmatrix}$ over $\mathbb{F}_{691}$: one eigenvalue, $667$, and not a scalar.
#   The entry 23 below the diagonal is the extension class of step 05. |
#   `hecke(H, p)` is the operator $T_p$ on $H^\bullet$ (for $p=\ell$ the same code computes $U_\ell$); `.A(q)` is its matrix on $H^q$ in the
#   stored basis, column convention. [C] `sec:operatordef`; validations `HeckeOperatorSmokeValidation`, `HeckeEndToEndValidation`. |
# | $\operatorname{char}(T_2)=(X+24)^3$. |
#   `charpoly` (Berkowitz, division-free) prints $X^3+72X^2+346X+4$; the code cell checks it equals $(X+24)^3$. |

# %% [md] 04 -- maximal ideals of the Hecke algebra and their characters
# Let $\mathbb{T}$ be the $\mathbb{F}_\ell$-algebra generated by the Hecke operators on $H^\bullet$. Each maximal ideal $\mathfrak{m}$ gives a system
# of eigenvalues, the character $\mathbb{T}\to\mathbb{T}/\mathfrak{m}=\mathbb{F}_{\ell^f}$, and $H^\bullet$ splits into the blocks $H_\mathfrak{m}$.
#
# > **At $(n,\ell)=(10,691)$** there is one maximal ideal, the Eisenstein ideal
# > $$\mathfrak{m}=(691,\ T_p-1-p^{11}),\qquad T_p\longmapsto 1+p^{11}\equiv\tau(p)\in\mathbb{F}_{691},$$
# > shared by $\Delta$ and $E_{12}$; its local algebra is $\mathbb{T}_\mathfrak{m}\cong\mathbb{F}_{691}[\varepsilon]/(\varepsilon^2)$, Hilbert function $(1,1)$.

# %% The maximal ideal of ext_record(10, 691)
# The coefficient ring is the default F_ell, stated here so that the file runs in any order in one session (08 sets Z).
set coefficient_ring = "F_ell";
R = ext_record(10, 691);
m = R.ideal(0);
display(m);
assert(R.num_ideals == 1 and m.local and m.residue_degree == 1 and m.dim == 3, "one local ideal, f = 1, dim H_m = 3");

# %% Its character
display(m.character(2), m.character(3));
assert(m.character(2) == poly(691, [(1 + 2^11) % 691]) and m.character(3) == poly(691, [(1 + 3^11) % 691]), "T_p -> 1 + p^11");
assert(m.character(3) == poly(691, [252]), "T_3 -> 252 = tau(3)");

# %% The local algebra
A = local_algebra(m);
display(A);
assert(A.dim == 2 and A.hilbert == [1, 1] and A.embdim == 1 and A.gorenstein, "T_m = F_691[eps]/eps^2");

# %% [md] Reading the output
# | Mathematics | What HACS computed, and how |
# |---|---|
# | One maximal ideal $\mathfrak{m}$, with residue field $\mathbb{F}_{691}$ ($f=1$), and $\dim H^1_\mathfrak{m}=3$: all of $H^1$. |
#   `ext_record(10, 691)` is the cell's `hecke_ext/v5` record; `.ideal(0)` the `MaximalIdeal` handle (`dim 3, f = 1, local`).
#   [E] `cl:ramanujan`; validations `HeckeExtRecordValidation`, `HeckeExtRamanujanValidation`. |
# | The character: $T_2\mapsto 667\equiv1+2^{11}$, $T_3\mapsto252=\tau(3)\equiv1+3^{11}$. |
#   `m.character(p)` is the eigenvalue of $T_p$ written as a polynomial in a witness $w$ of the residue field; for $f=1$ a constant. |
# | $\mathbb{T}_\mathfrak{m}\cong\mathbb{F}_{691}[\varepsilon]/(\varepsilon^2)$, $\varepsilon$ the image of $T_2+24$: local, Gorenstein, not reduced. |
#   `local_algebra(m)`: `dim 2 over F_691, f = 1, embdim 1, nu = 2, Gorenstein`; `hilbert` $=(1,1)$ is $\dim\mathfrak{m}^i/\mathfrak{m}^{i+1}$. |

# %% [md] 05 -- how H_m sits over T_m: Loewy layers, socle layers, Jordan types
# The block $H^1_\mathfrak{m}$ is a module over the local algebra $\mathbb{T}_\mathfrak{m}$ of step 04. Its **Loewy layers**
# $\mathfrak{m}^jH/\mathfrak{m}^{j+1}H$ and **socle layers** measure how far it is from semisimple.
#
# > **At $(10,691)$.** $H^1_\mathfrak{m}$ is a non-split extension of the Eisenstein line by the cusp part:
# > $$0\to H^1_!\to H^1_\mathfrak{m}\to H^1_\partial\to0\ \text{ does not split},\qquad \text{Loewy layers } 2\mid1,\qquad \text{Jordan type of } T_p-(1+p^{11}):\ (2,1).$$
#
# This is the sequence of step 02, now seen from the Hecke algebra: $T_p-(1+p^{11})$ is nilpotent on $H^1_\mathfrak{m}$ but not zero.

# %% Setting and the maximal ideal
# The coefficient ring is the default F_ell, stated here so that the file runs in any order in one session (08 sets Z).
set coefficient_ring = "F_ell";
R = ext_record(10, 691);
m = R.ideal(0);

# %% The Loewy profile
P = module_structure(m);
display(P);
assert(P.D == 3 and P.loewy == [2, 1] and P.socle == [2, 1] and not P.semisimple, "Loewy layers 2 | 1: not semisimple");

# %% The Jordan type of T_2
display(P.jordan(2));
assert(P.jordan(2) == [2, 1], "Jordan type (2, 1)");

# %% [md] Reading the output
# | Mathematics | What HACS computed, and how |
# |---|---|
# | $H^1_\mathfrak{m}/\mathfrak{m}H^1_\mathfrak{m}$ has dimension 2 and $\mathfrak{m}H^1_\mathfrak{m}$ dimension 1, so $H^1_\mathfrak{m}$ needs two
#   generators and has Loewy length 2: it is not semisimple, so the extension does not split. |
#   `module_structure(m)`: the `LoewyProfile` of $H^1_\mathfrak{m}$, printed `loewy [2, 1], socle [2, 1], not cyclic` (dimensions in units of
#   $f=1$). `semisimple` is false. [E] `alg:stage5`; validation `HeckeExtAlgebraInvariantsValidation`. |
# | $T_2-667$ has Jordan type $(2,1)$: one block of size 2 (the extension) and one of size 1. |
#   `P.jordan(p)`: the Jordan type of the nilpotent part $n_p=t_p-P_p(\hat w)$ on the block. |

# %% [md] 06 -- the Dickson long exact sequence
# Over $\mathbb{F}_\ell$ ($\ell\ge5$), multiplication by the Dickson invariant $\theta = XY^\ell - X^\ell Y$ gives a short exact sequence of
# $\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z})$-modules, with $n'=n+\ell+1$,
#
# $$0\longrightarrow\mathrm{Sym}^n\otimes\det\xrightarrow{\ \theta\ }\mathrm{Sym}^{n'}\longrightarrow Q_n\longrightarrow0,$$
#
# hence a six-term sequence in cohomology:
#
# > $$0\to H^0(\mathrm{Sym}^n\!\otimes\!\det)\xrightarrow{\theta^0}H^0(\mathrm{Sym}^{n'})\xrightarrow{\pi^0}H^0(Q_n)\xrightarrow{\delta}H^1(\mathrm{Sym}^n\!\otimes\!\det)\xrightarrow{\theta^1}H^1(\mathrm{Sym}^{n'})\xrightarrow{\pi^1}H^1(Q_n)\to0 .$$
# > At $(n,\ell)=(16,7)$, so $n'=24$, the dimensions are $1,1,1,4,4,1$ and the ranks of the five maps are $1,0,1,3,1$.

# %% The sequence and its five maps
# The coefficient ring is the default F_ell, stated here so that the file runs in any order in one session (08 sets Z).
set coefficient_ring = "F_ell";
L = dickson_les(16, 7);
display(L);

# %% Dimensions and exactness
display(L.dims, L.exact);
d = L.dims;
assert([d.H0_n, d.H0_up, d.H0_Q, d.H1_n, d.H1_up, d.H1_Q] == [1, 1, 1, 4, 4, 1], "dimensions 1, 1, 1, 4, 4, 1");
r = L.ranks;
assert([r.theta0, r.pi0, r.delta, r.theta1, r.pi1] == [1, 0, 1, 3, 1] and L.exact and L.ker_equals_im, "ranks 1, 0, 1, 3, 1; exact");

# %% [md] Reading the output
# | Mathematics | What HACS computed, and how |
# |---|---|
# | The sequence is exact at all six places. With the dimensions $1,1,1,4,4,1$ the ranks are forced: $\theta^0$ is injective,
#   so $\pi^0=0$ and $\delta$ is injective; $\ker\theta^1=\operatorname{im}\delta$ is a line, so $\theta^1$ has rank 3 and $\pi^1$ is onto. |
#   `dickson_les(16, 7)`: the six terms (`dims`: `H0_n`, `H0_up`, `H0_Q`, `H1_n`, `H1_up`, `H1_Q`), the five maps in the stored bases (each
#   typeset with its source and target), `ranks`, and `exact` (exactness certified as subspaces). [E] `sec:thetaobj`; validation `HeckeDicksonThetaValidation`. |
# | $H^0(\mathrm{Sym}^{24}\mathbb{F}_7^2)\neq0$: an invariant of $\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{F}_7)$ in degree $24=7+1+16$. |
#   `dickson_count` $=\#\{(a,b):a(\ell+1)+b(\ell^2-\ell)=n\}$ counts the Dickson monomials of degree $n$. |

# %% [md] 07 -- from F_ell to Z/ell^K: the Hecke algebra over a ring
# Over $\mathbb{F}_\ell$ a block only sees a system of eigenvalues mod $\ell$. Over $\mathbb{Z}/\ell^K$ it sees the lattice: for each block
# (maximal ideal $\mathfrak{m}$) the localised cohomology is
#
# > $$\Lambda_\mathfrak{m}\cong\mathbb{Z}_\ell^{\,f}\oplus\bigoplus_i\mathbb{Z}/\ell^{e_i},$$
# > a free part of rank $f$ and torsion. With $A_1=\mathbb{T}_\mathfrak{m}/\ell$ and $A_K=\mathbb{T}_\mathfrak{m}/\ell^K$, the Hilbert functions of
# > $A_1$ and $A_K$ say how non-reduced the algebra is and whether that is stable in $K$.
#
# At $(n,\ell)=(48,7)$ there are four blocks; one has rank 4 with Hilbert function $(1,1)$ (a congruence), and one is pure torsion, $\Lambda_\mathfrak{m}\cong\mathbb{Z}/7$.

# %% The module route over Z/7^2
# The coefficient ring is the default F_ell, stated here so that the file runs in any order in one session (08 sets Z).
set coefficient_ring = "F_ell";
R = hecke_ring_algebra(48, 7, 2);

# %% Blocks, lattices, Hilbert functions
display(R);
assert(len(R.blocks) == 4, "four blocks");
assert(R.blocks[0].free_rank == 4 and R.blocks[0].torsion == [] and R.blocks[0].A1_hilbert == [1, 1] and R.blocks[0].verdict == "stable", "block 0: Z_7^4, A_1 = (1, 1), stable");
assert(R.blocks[2].free_rank == 0 and R.blocks[2].torsion == [1], "block 2: Z/7, pure torsion");

# %% [md] Reading the output
# | Mathematics | What HACS computed, and how |
# |---|---|
# | Block 0: $\Lambda_\mathfrak{m}\cong\mathbb{Z}_7^4$, $A_1$ and $A_K$ with Hilbert function $(1,1)$: a non-reduced algebra, stable from $K=2$ on. |
#   `hecke_ring_algebra(48, 7, 2)`: per block `system` (the eigenvalues mod 7 of $T_2,T_3,T_5,T_7$), `D`, `free_rank`, `torsion` (the $e_i$),
#   `A1_hilbert`, `AK_hilbert`, `K_stab`, `verdict`. [C] `cor:stablecert`; validation `HeckeRingLoewyValidation`. |
# | Block 2: $\Lambda_\mathfrak{m}\cong\mathbb{Z}/7$, a system of eigenvalues mod 7 that lifts to no eigenform of the same weight and level in characteristic 0. |
#   `free_rank: 0, torsion: [1]`, `verdict: "torsion_stable"`. Tours 08–10 read such torsion over $\mathbb{Z}$. |

# %% [md] 08 -- over Z: the integral cohomology as abelian groups
# With `coefficient_ring = "Z"` HACS computes $H^q(\Gamma_0(N),\mathrm{Sym}^n\mathbb{Z}^2)$ as finitely generated abelian groups
# $\mathbb{Z}^r\oplus\bigoplus(\mathbb{Z}/p^k)^m$, and the characteristic polynomial $\operatorname{char}_\mathbb{Z}(T_p)$ of $T_p$ on the free part, over $\mathbb{Z}$.
#
# > **At level 1, $n=10$.**
# > $$H^1(\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z}),\mathrm{Sym}^{10}\mathbb{Z}^2)\cong\mathbb{Z}^3\oplus\mathbb{Z}/2\oplus\mathbb{Z}/4\oplus\mathbb{Z}/3,$$
# > $$\begin{aligned} \operatorname{char}_{\mathbb{Z}}(T_2) &= (X+24)^2\,(X-2049) = (X-\tau(2))^2\,(X-\sigma_{11}(2)),\\ \operatorname{char}_{\mathbb{Z}}(T_3) &= (X-252)^2\,(X-177148),\\ \operatorname{char}_{\mathbb{Z}}(T_5) &= (X-4830)^2\,(X-48828126). \end{aligned}$$
# > The double root is $\tau(p)$ ($\Delta$, both signs), the simple root $\sigma_{11}(p)=1+p^{11}$ ($E_{12}$); the torsion primes 2 and 3 divide
# > the orders of the stabilisers.

# %% Descent at level 1, n = 10
set coefficient_ring = "Z";
D = descent(1, 10);
display(D);

# %% The integral groups
display(D.groups_inv);
assert(D.groups[1] == "Z^3 + Z/2 + Z/2^2 + Z/3", "H^1 = Z^3 + Z/2 + Z/4 + Z/3");

# %% char_Z(T_p), factored over Z
display(D.charpoly_factored);
assert(D.charpoly_factored[0].factored == "(X + 24)^2 (X - 2049)", "char_Z(T_2)");
assert(D.charpoly_factored[1].factored == "(X - 252)^2 (X - 177148)", "char_Z(T_3)");
assert(D.charpoly_factored[2].factored == "(X - 4830)^2 (X - 48828126)", "char_Z(T_5)");
assert(1 + 2^11 == 2049 and 1 + 3^11 == 177148 and 1 + 5^11 == 48828126, "the simple roots are 1 + p^11");

# %% [md] Reading the output
# | Mathematics | What HACS computed, and how |
# |---|---|
# | $H^q$ over $\mathbb{Z}$ for $q=0,\dots,3$: $0$, $\mathbb{Z}^3\oplus\mathbb{Z}/2\oplus\mathbb{Z}/4\oplus\mathbb{Z}/3$, $\mathbb{Z}/2$, $(\mathbb{Z}/2)^5\oplus\mathbb{Z}/3$
#   (higher degrees are periodic). |
#   `descent(1, 10)`: the record `integral_descent_record/v1`; `groups_inv` per degree `{free_rank, torsion: [[p, k, m], ...]}`
#   $=\mathbb{Z}^r\oplus\bigoplus(\mathbb{Z}/p^k)^m$; `groups` the same as text (`Z^3 + Z/2 + Z/2^2 + Z/3`). [C] `thm:descentcert`; validation `IntegralDescentGroupsValidation`. |
# | $\operatorname{char}_\mathbb{Z}(T_p)=(X-\tau(p))^2(X-\sigma_{11}(p))$ for $p=2,3,5$; the roots differ by multiples of 691 (Ramanujan, tour 10). |
#   `charpoly_factored`: per $p$ the expanded polynomial, its factorisation over $\mathbb{Z}$ (Yun + Zassenhaus, exact), and `verified: true`
#   (the product of the factors equals $\operatorname{char}_\mathbb{Z}$). Validation `IntegralDescentRecordValidation`, arm (Z). |

# %% [md] 09 -- over Z: the Hecke operators as integer matrices, torsion included
# Write $H^1=H^1(\Gamma_0(N),\mathrm{Sym}^n\mathbb{Z}^2)=T\oplus\mathbb{Z}^r$ with $T$ the torsion. In generators "torsion first, then a basis
# $B=(b_1,\dots,b_r)$ of a free complement", every Hecke operator is block triangular:
#
# > $$T_p=\begin{pmatrix}\tau_p & M_p\\ 0 & F_p\end{pmatrix},\qquad T_p\,b_j=\sum_i (F_p)_{ij}\,b_i+\sum_a (M_p)_{aj}\,g_a ,$$
# > $\tau_p$ on $T$, $F_p\in M_r(\mathbb{Z})$ on the free part (exact integers), and $M_p$ recording how free generators move into the torsion
# > ($g_a$ the torsion generators). $B$ is certified to be a $\mathbb{Z}$-basis by one determinant, $\det(\Phi^{\top}B)=\pm1$.
#
# At level 1 and $n=30$: $T\cong(\mathbb{Z}/2)^3\oplus(\mathbb{Z}/4)^3\oplus(\mathbb{Z}/3)^2\oplus\mathbb{Z}/9\oplus\mathbb{Z}/25\oplus\mathbb{Z}/29$ and $r=5$;
# the entries of $F_3$ have up to 89 bits. The matrices are a presentation, unique up to the block-triangular group; $M$ depends on the
# choice of complement (tour 10 says what decides splitting).

# %% H^1 over Z at level 1, n = 30
set coefficient_ring = "Z";
D = descent(1, 30, [2, 3]);
Z = D.module_Z();
display(Z.invariant_factors);
display(Z.basis);
assert(Z.free_rank == 5 and Z.invariant_factors == [2, 2, 2, 4, 4, 4, 3, 3, 9, 25, 29], "T and r = 5");
assert(Z.basis.saturation == "det_pm1", "B certified by det = +-1");

# %% T_3: the free block F over Z and its characteristic polynomial
display(Z.ops[1].F);
display(Z.ops[1].charpoly_Z);
assert(Z.ops[1].p == 3, "T_3");

# %% T_3: the torsion block tau and the mixed block M (per torsion prime, against the classes of B)
display(Z.ops[1].tau);
display(Z.ops[1].M);

# %% The torsion coordinates of the basis B at ell = 5 (pi_B: one row per generator of the 5-torsion)
display(Z.retractions[2].ell);
display(Z.retractions[2].pi_B);
assert(Z.retractions[2].ell == 5, "the 5-torsion");

# %% [md] Reading the output
# | Mathematics | What HACS computed, and how |
# |---|---|
# | The torsion $T$ by its elementary divisors, and a $\mathbb{Z}$-basis $B$ of a free complement. |
#   `module_Z()`: `invariant_factors` (the $d_i$), `basis` = `{rule: "iso_dixon/v3", saturation: "det_pm1", ...}`: $B$ is picked by Dixon
#   lifting on the free tree model and certified by $\det(\Phi_{\mathrm{cert}}^\top B)=\pm1$. [C] `prop:torsionhecke`; validation `HeckeZExportZ2Validation`. |
# | $F_3\in M_5(\mathbb{Z})$ and its characteristic polynomial, the same as the record's $\operatorname{char}_\mathbb{Z}(T_3)$. |
#   `ops[1].F` (decimal strings, any size: the CRT over many primes is proven), `ops[1].charpoly_Z` (high to low). |
# | $\tau_3$ and $M_3$: the action on the torsion and the mixed block. |
#   `ops[1].tau` (row $i$ mod $d_i$), `ops[1].M` per torsion prime $\ell$. Changing the complement changes $M$ by $\pi_BF-\tau\pi_B$, where
#   $\pi_B$ (`retractions[].pi_B`) gives the torsion coordinates of the $b_j$. |

# %% [md] 10 -- over Z: congruences and the torsion-by-free extension, as certified statements
# Tour 09 gave the Hecke operators over $\mathbb{Z}$ as matrices. The record `hecke_structure(N, n)` says what they do to the Hecke algebra.
# Let $\mathbb{T}$ be the ring generated by the $T_p$ on the free part $L=H^1/\mathrm{tors}$. At level 1, $L\otimes\mathbb{Q}$ splits into an
# Eisenstein component and cuspidal components, and the **congruence module** $C_\mathbb{T}=\mathbb{T}/(I_{\mathrm{Eis}}+I_{\mathrm{cusp}})$ measures
# how they are glued.
#
# > **Ramanujan over $\mathbb{Z}$ (level 1, $n=10$).**
# > $$\mathbb{T}\;\cong\;\mathbb{Z}\times_{\mathbb{Z}/691}\mathbb{Z}=\{(a,b): a\equiv b\bmod 691\},\qquad T_n\longmapsto(\sigma_{11}(n),\tau(n)),\qquad C_\mathbb{T}\cong\mathbb{Z}/691 .$$
# > Locally at 691: $\mathbb{T}_\mathfrak{m}\cong\mathbb{Z}_{691}[x]/(x(x-691))$, Gorenstein, $\mathfrak{m}=(691,T_2+24)$; and $v_{691}(B_{12}/12)=1$ agrees.
#
# > **Level 1, $n=30$ (weight 32).** $C_\mathbb{T}\cong\mathbb{Z}/7709321041217$, and $7709321041217=37\cdot683\cdot305065927$ is the numerator of
# > the Bernoulli number $B_{32}$: the Eisenstein congruences of weight 32 (Kummer, Herbrand–Ribet), all at once.
#
# The record's fields are certified values or named statuses, and the record as a whole is `final` or `pending`. The records and the
# descent of this file take a few seconds and about 0.25 GiB together.

# %% Ramanujan over Z: the record of (1, 10)
set coefficient_ring = "Z";
R = hecke_structure(1, 10);
display(R.status, R.C_T);
assert(R.status == "final" and R.C_T == "certified:0x2b3" and 0x2b3 == 691, "C_T = Z/691, final");
fields = ["G3.C_T.exact=0x2b3", "G3.fibre_product=certified", "G3.second_route.ell=691.v_Bk=1", "G3.second_route.ell=691.agrees=true",
          "G2.ell=691.m[0].residue_poly=[24,1]", "G2.ell=691.m[0].local_type.kind=monogenic", "G2.ell=691.m[0].local_type.gorenstein=true",
          "G2.ell=691.m[0].local_type.hilbert=1,1", "G6.q=1.ell=2.pt[0].split=split_on_P", "G6.q=1.ell=3.pt[0].split=split_on_P"];
for w in fields { k = 0; for s in R.body { if s == w { k = k + 1 } }; assert(k == 1, w); }
display(fields);

# %% Weight 32: C_T is the numerator of B_32
R30 = hecke_structure(1, 30);
display(R30.status, R30.C_T);
assert(R30.status == "final" and R30.C_T == "certified:0x702f7579541", "C_T at (1, 30)");
assert(0x702f7579541 == 7709321041217 and 37 * 683 * 305065927 == 7709321041217, "= 37 * 683 * 305065927");

# %% [md] Reading the output
# | Mathematics | What HACS computed, and how |
# |---|---|
# | $C_\mathbb{T}\cong\mathbb{Z}/691$ and $\mathbb{T}\cong\mathbb{Z}\times_{\mathbb{Z}/691}\mathbb{Z}$: one ring statement equivalent to $\tau(n)\equiv\sigma_{11}(n)\bmod691$
#   for all $n$ (for $T_2$: $2049+24=3\cdot691$). |
#   `C_T = "certified:0x2b3"`: the record stores integers in signed hexadecimal ($\mathtt{0x2b3}=691$); the page shows the decimal and,
#   on hover, the stored form. "certified" = the lower and upper bounds for $|C_\mathbb{T}|$ meet. `G3.fibre_product=certified`.
#   [C] `def:heckeZcong`, `prop:heckeZcong` (3), (4); validation `HeckeZRecordValidation`. |
# | Locally at 691: one maximal ideal $\mathfrak{m}$, residue polynomial $X+24$, $\mathbb{T}_\mathfrak{m}$ monogenic of rank 2, Gorenstein,
#   Hilbert function $(1,1)$; the second route $v_{691}(B_{12}/12)=1$ agrees. |
#   `G2.ell=691.m[0]...` (`residue_poly=[24,1]` lists coefficients low to high), `G3.second_route.ell=691.v_Bk=1`, `agrees=true`. |
# | At weight 32, $C_\mathbb{T}\cong\mathbb{Z}/(37\cdot683\cdot305065927)$, the numerator of $B_{32}$. |
#   `certified:0x702f7579541`; per prime `G3.C_T.ell=37/683/305065927` with lower = upper valuation 1. The Bernoulli cross-check
#   (`second_route`) ran at 37 and 683 and agrees; at 305065927 it reads `not_run`. |

# %% [md] Torsion-by-free extensions: where the torsion does not split off
# Fix a prime $\ell$; let $T_\ell$ be the $\ell$-torsion of $H^1$, of exponent $e_\ell$, and $L_\ell=L\otimes\mathbb{Z}_\ell$. The Hecke modules sit in
#
# > $$0\longrightarrow T_\ell\longrightarrow H^1_\ell\longrightarrow L_\ell\longrightarrow0,$$
# > and the question is whether a complement of $T_\ell$ is stable under $\mathbb{T}$. In a basis adapted to the torsion,
# > $T_p=\begin{pmatrix}B_p&0\\ \beta_p&c_p\end{pmatrix}$, and a complement is the graph of some $h\in\mathrm{Hom}(L/\ell^{e_\ell},T_\ell)$; it is stable under the
# > computed $T_p$ iff $h B_p-c_ph=\beta_p$ for all of them. **Unsolvable for the computed subalgebra $\mathbb{T}'$ implies non-split for $\mathbb{T}$**:
# > a certificate ([C] `prop:heckeZglue` (2)(b)).
#
# So `non_split` at a torsion point is a theorem about $\mathbb{T}$. `split_on_P` is weaker: split for $\mathbb{T}'=\mathbb{Z}[T_p : p\in P]$ only; split for
# all of $\mathbb{T}$ would need [C] `rmk:heckeZcbthree`, a conjecture. A basis-free class in $\mathrm{Ext}^1$ is not computed: the record stores the verdict.

# %% Level 16, n = 2: non_split at 2
R = hecke_structure(16, 2);
display(R.status, R.signature);
for w in ["G6.q=1.ell=2.pt[0].split=non_split", "G5.q=1.ell=2.pt[0].route=nonsplit"] { k = 0; for s in R.body { if s == w { k = k + 1 } }; assert(k == 1, w); }

# %% Level 27, n = 2: non_split at 3, split_on_P at 2
R = hecke_structure(27, 2);
display(R.status, R.signature);
for w in ["G6.q=1.ell=3.pt[0].split=non_split", "G6.q=1.ell=2.pt[0].split=split_on_P"] { k = 0; for s in R.body { if s == w { k = k + 1 } }; assert(k == 1, w); }

# %% Level 8, n = 4: non_split at 2, split_on_P at 3
R = hecke_structure(8, 4);
display(R.status, R.signature);
for w in ["G6.q=1.ell=2.pt[0].split=non_split", "G6.q=1.ell=3.pt[0].split=split_on_P"] { k = 0; for s in R.body { if s == w { k = k + 1 } }; assert(k == 1, w); }
assert(R.status == "final", "final");

# %% [md] Reading the output
# | Mathematics | What HACS computed, and how |
# |---|---|
# | $\Gamma_0(16)$, $n=2$: $H^1\cong\mathbb{Z}^{12}\oplus\mathbb{Z}/2\oplus\mathbb{Z}/8$, and its 2-torsion has **no** Hecke-stable complement. |
#   `G6=[2.pt[0]=non_split]` in the signature; `G5...route=nonsplit`, `relation_to_free=common_certified`: the certificate also shows that this
#   torsion system equals a free one for all $p$ ([C] `prop:heckeZglue` (4), `thm:heckeZdescent` (5)(ii)). |
# | $\Gamma_0(27)$, $n=2$: non-split at 3; at 2 split for $\mathbb{T}'$. $\Gamma_0(8)$, $n=4$: non-split at 2; at 3 split for $\mathbb{T}'$. |
#   `split_on_P` = split for the subalgebra generated by the computed $T_p$. These records are non-reduced (`redQ=non_reduced_certified`), so
#   `C_T` reads `not_run`; the `non_split` verdicts use only $T_p$ with $p\nmid N$ and do not wait for that. |

# %% [md] Against tour 09: the block M is not the verdict
# Tour 09 printed $T_p=\begin{pmatrix}\tau&M\\0&F\end{pmatrix}$ on $H^1=T\oplus\mathbb{Z}^r$. $M$ depends on the chosen complement: another complement changes it
# by $\pi_BF-\tau\pi_B$, the same shape as $hB_p-c_ph$ above. So $M\neq0$ proves nothing, and `module_Z()` does not decide (its field `split` is
# `unknown`); the record decides. At $(1,30)$, $M$ of $T_2$ is non-zero at 2, 3 and 29, and yet the record reads `split_on_P` at 2, 3 and 5,
# and `split_by_support` at 29 (the 29-torsion lives on a system of eigenvalues different from the free ones).

# %% module_Z at (1, 30): M of T_2 is printed, split is unknown
D = descent(1, 30, [2, 3]);
Z = D.module_Z();
display(Z.split);
display(Z.ops[0].M);
assert(Z.split == "unknown", "module_Z does not decide");

# %% The record of the same cell decides
display(R30.signature);
for w in ["G6.q=1.ell=29.pt[0].split=split_by_support", "G5.q=1.ell=29.pt[0].relation_to_free=distinct_certified"] { k = 0; for s in R30.body { if s == w { k = k + 1 } }; assert(k == 1, w); }

# %% [md] The full record (optional)
# The last cell prints the whole hashed body of the record at $(1,10)$, sections G0 to G8, one `key=value` line each ([C] `sec:recordschema`,
# item 8). The cells above read the lines that carry the mathematics.

# %% The full body of the record (1, 10)
display(hecke_structure(1, 10).body);
